{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"While Svelte v5 has legacy compatibility with the stores syntax from Svelte v3/v4, it has been somewhat buggy and unreliable for this adapter. The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/svelte-query"},{"type":"text","value":" v6 adapter fully migrates to the runes syntax, which relies on signals. This rewrite should also simplify the code required to ensure your query inputs remain reactive."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"installation","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Installation"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Please ensure your project has "},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/sveltejs/svelte/releases/tag/svelte%405.25.0","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Svelte v5.25.0"}]},{"type":"text","value":" or newer."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Run "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pnpm add @tanstack/svelte-query@latest"},{"type":"text","value":" (or your package manager's equivalent)."}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note that "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/svelte-query"},{"type":"text","value":" v6 depends on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/query-core"},{"type":"text","value":" v5."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"function-inputs","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Function Inputs"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Like the Angular and Solid adapters, most functions for the Svelte adapter now require options to be provided as a \"thunk\" ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"() => options"},{"type":"text","value":") to provide reactivity. TypeScript will be your friend here and warn you if you're missing this notation."}]},{"type":"code","value":"- const query = createQuery({  // [!code --]\n+ const query = createQuery(() => ({ // [!code ++]\n    queryKey: ['todos'],\n    queryFn: () => fetchTodos(),\n- }) // [!code --]\n+ })) // [!code ++]","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"accessing-properties","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Accessing Properties"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Given the adapter no longer uses stores, it is no longer necessary to prefix with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"$"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"code","value":"- {#if $todos.isSuccess} // [!code --]\n+ {#if todos.isSuccess} // [!code ++]\n    <ul>\n-     {#each $todos.data.items as item} // [!code --]\n+     {#each todos.data.items as item} // [!code ++]\n        <li>{item}</li>\n      {/each}\n    </ul>\n  {/if}","lang":"svelte"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"reactivity","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reactivity"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You previously needed to do some funky things with stores to achieve reactivity for inputs. This is no longer the case! You don't even need to wrap your query in a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"$derived"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"code","value":"- const intervalMs = writable(1000) // [!code --]\n+ let intervalMs = $state(1000) // [!code ++]\n\n- const query = createQuery(derived(intervalMs, ($intervalMs) => ({ // [!code --]\n+ const query = createQuery(() => ({ // [!code ++]\n    queryKey: ['refetch'],\n    queryFn: async () => await fetch('/api/data').then((r) => r.json()),\n-   refetchInterval: $intervalMs, // [!code --]\n+   refetchInterval: intervalMs, // [!code ++]\n- }))) // [!code --]\n+ })) // [!code ++]","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"disabling-legacy-mode","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Disabling Legacy Mode"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If your component has any stores, it might not properly switch to runes mode. You can ensure your application is using runes in two ways:"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"on-a-per-file-basis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On a per-file basis"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In each "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".svelte"},{"type":"text","value":" file, once you have migrated to runes, add "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"<svelte:options runes={true} />"},{"type":"text","value":". This is better for large applications requiring gradual migration."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"on-a-project-wide-basis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On a project-wide basis"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In your "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"svelte.config.js"},{"type":"text","value":", add the following to config:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"  compilerOptions: {\n    runes: true,\n  },","lang":"json"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This can be added once you've 100% eradicated stores syntax from your app."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"installation","text":"Installation","level":2},{"id":"function-inputs","text":"Function Inputs","level":2},{"id":"accessing-properties","text":"Accessing Properties","level":2},{"id":"reactivity","text":"Reactivity","level":2},{"id":"disabling-legacy-mode","text":"Disabling Legacy Mode","level":2},{"id":"on-a-per-file-basis","text":"On a per-file basis","level":3},{"id":"on-a-project-wide-basis","text":"On a project-wide basis","level":3}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"While Svelte v5 has legacy compatibility with the stores syntax from Svelte v3/v4, it has been somewhat buggy and unreliable for this adapter. The `@tanstack/svelte-query` v6 adapter fully migrates to the runes syntax, which relies on signals. This rewrite should also simplify the code required to ensure your query inputs remain reactive.\n\n## Installation\n\nPlease ensure your project has [Svelte v5.25.0](https://github.com/sveltejs/svelte/releases/tag/svelte%405.25.0) or newer.\n\nRun `pnpm add @tanstack/svelte-query@latest` (or your package manager's equivalent).\n\n> Note that `@tanstack/svelte-query` v6 depends on `@tanstack/query-core` v5.\n\n## Function Inputs\n\nLike the Angular and Solid adapters, most functions for the Svelte adapter now require options to be provided as a \"thunk\" (`() => options`) to provide reactivity. TypeScript will be your friend here and warn you if you're missing this notation.\n\n```ts\n- const query = createQuery({  // [!code --]\n+ const query = createQuery(() => ({ // [!code ++]\n    queryKey: ['todos'],\n    queryFn: () => fetchTodos(),\n- }) // [!code --]\n+ })) // [!code ++]\n```\n\n## Accessing Properties\n\nGiven the adapter no longer uses stores, it is no longer necessary to prefix with `$`.\n\n```svelte\n- {#if $todos.isSuccess} // [!code --]\n+ {#if todos.isSuccess} // [!code ++]\n    <ul>\n-     {#each $todos.data.items as item} // [!code --]\n+     {#each todos.data.items as item} // [!code ++]\n        <li>{item}</li>\n      {/each}\n    </ul>\n  {/if}\n```\n\n## Reactivity\n\nYou previously needed to do some funky things with stores to achieve reactivity for inputs. This is no longer the case! You don't even need to wrap your query in a `$derived`.\n\n```ts\n- const intervalMs = writable(1000) // [!code --]\n+ let intervalMs = $state(1000) // [!code ++]\n\n- const query = createQuery(derived(intervalMs, ($intervalMs) => ({ // [!code --]\n+ const query = createQuery(() => ({ // [!code ++]\n    queryKey: ['refetch'],\n    queryFn: async () => await fetch('/api/data').then((r) => r.json()),\n-   refetchInterval: $intervalMs, // [!code --]\n+   refetchInterval: intervalMs, // [!code ++]\n- }))) // [!code --]\n+ })) // [!code ++]\n```\n\n## Disabling Legacy Mode\n\nIf your component has any stores, it might not properly switch to runes mode. You can ensure your application is using runes in two ways:\n\n### On a per-file basis\n\nIn each `.svelte` file, once you have migrated to runes, add `<svelte:options runes={true} />`. This is better for large applications requiring gradual migration.\n\n### On a project-wide basis\n\nIn your `svelte.config.js`, add the following to config:\n\n```json\n  compilerOptions: {\n    runes: true,\n  },\n```\n\nThis can be added once you've 100% eradicated stores syntax from your app.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"While Svelte v5 has legacy compatibility with the stores syntax from Svelte v3/v4, it has been somewhat buggy and unreliable for this adapter. The `@tanstack/svelte-query` v6 adapter fully migrates to the runes syntax, which relies on signals. This rewrite should also simplify the code required to ensure your query inputs remain reactive.\n\n## Installation\n\nPlease ensure your project has [Svelte v5.25.0](https://github.com/sveltejs/svelte/releases/tag/svelte%405.25.0) or newer.\n\nRun `pnpm add @tanstack/svelte-query@latest` (or your package manager's equivalent).\n\n> Note that `@tanstack/svelte-query` v6 depends on `@tanstack/query-core` v5.\n\n## Function Inputs\n\nLike the Angular and Solid adapters, most functions for the Svelte adapter now require options to be provided as a \"thunk\" (`() => options`) to provide reactivity. TypeScript will be your friend here and warn you if you're missing this notation.\n\n```ts\n- const query = createQuery({  // [!code --]\n+ const query = createQuery(() => ({ // [!code ++]\n    queryKey: ['todos'],\n    queryFn: () => fetchTodos(),\n- }) // [!code --]\n+ })) // [!code ++]\n```\n\n## Accessing Properties\n\nGiven the adapter no longer uses stores, it is no longer necessary to prefix with `$`.\n\n```svelte\n- {#if $todos.isSuccess} // [!code --]\n+ {#if todos.isSuccess} // [!code ++]\n    <ul>\n-     {#each $todos.data.items as item} // [!code --]\n+     {#each todos.data.items as item} // [!code ++]\n        <li>{item}</li>\n      {/each}\n    </ul>\n  {/if}\n```\n\n## Reactivity\n\nYou previously needed to do some funky things with stores to achieve reactivity for inputs. This is no longer the case! You don't even need to wrap your query in a `$derived`.\n\n```ts\n- const intervalMs = writable(1000) // [!code --]\n+ let intervalMs = $state(1000) // [!code ++]\n\n- const query = createQuery(derived(intervalMs, ($intervalMs) => ({ // [!code --]\n+ const query = createQuery(() => ({ // [!code ++]\n    queryKey: ['refetch'],\n    queryFn: async () => await fetch('/api/data').then((r) => r.json()),\n-   refetchInterval: $intervalMs, // [!code --]\n+   refetchInterval: intervalMs, // [!code ++]\n- }))) // [!code --]\n+ })) // [!code ++]\n```\n\n## Disabling Legacy Mode\n\nIf your component has any stores, it might not properly switch to runes mode. You can ensure your application is using runes in two ways:\n\n### On a per-file basis\n\nIn each `.svelte` file, once you have migrated to runes, add `<svelte:options runes={true} />`. This is better for large applications requiring gradual migration.\n\n### On a project-wide basis\n\nIn your `svelte.config.js`, add the following to config:\n\n```json\n  compilerOptions: {\n    runes: true,\n  },\n```\n\nThis can be added once you've 100% eradicated stores syntax from your app.\n"}
